# Where did this whole enterprise come from?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a clear, warm university lecturer: measured, engaged, never theatrical","b":"gemini:Charon | a clear, warm university lecturer: measured, engaged, never theatrical"}
next: {"title":"Norway's priority-setting system, criterion by criterion","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/health-technology-assessment-deciding/norway-s-priority-setting-system.cast"}

## Cochrane's Awkward Question
voice: male
level: basic

    box root "A new treatment" hidden true
    box q_works "Does it work?" #2f6b8f hidden true
    box q_all "Can everyone get it?" #b5482e hidden true
    arrow arr1 root -> q_works curved #2f6b8f hidden true
    label lbl_eff "Effectiveness" above #2f6b8f attach_to arr1 hidden true
    arrow arr2 root -> q_all curved #b5482e hidden true
    label lbl_effic "Efficiency" below #b5482e attach_to arr2 hidden true

Three moments built this field — an idea, an institution and a scandal. Today, the idea: one awkward question asked about any new treatment.
    draw root

The first question medicine has always asked is this one — does it work? Effectiveness.
    draw arr1 lbl_eff q_works

    pause 0.3

The second, it mostly didn't ask: could a health service actually give it to everyone who might benefit? Efficiency.
    draw arr2 lbl_effic q_all delivery grave

    pause 0.5

The man who put those two side by side was Archie Cochrane, in a slim 1972 book called Effectiveness and Efficiency.
    portrait cochrane of "Archie Cochrane" url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/04/Archie_Cochrane.webp cameo true

He came to it the hard way. As a young doctor he spent the war as a prisoner, medical officer in German prisoner-of-war camps, treating hundreds of sick men with almost no drugs.
    delivery soft

He wrote later that he had never heard of a randomised trial then — but he knew there was no real evidence that anything he offered helped, and he feared he had shortened some of his friends' lives by treating them.
    delivery soft

So when he wrote his book he wanted evidence — and then he wanted something more on top of it.
    erase cochrane

His case in point, and ours, is a piece of technology every hospital wanted: the coronary care unit.
    label case_label "Case: the coronary care unit, 1971" above attach_to root

Through the sixties these units spread fast — monitors, defibrillators, trained staff — on the entirely reasonable assumption that a heart attack is safer in hospital than at home.
    highlight q_works effect glow

Then a trial in Bristol randomised several hundred men with suspected heart attacks either to a coronary care unit or to their own bed, looked after by their family doctor. Survival in hospital was no better.
    label ccu_works "No better than home" below #2f6b8f attach_to q_works
    delivery grave

Cochrane liked to tell how he first showed cardiologists those results with the two columns swapped — they demanded the home-care arm be stopped at once. Then he handed them the real table, and, well… the room went quiet.
    point at.ref ccu_works gesture underline

    pause 0.4

Now his second question, the one that makes this a field about money: a coronary care unit in every district hospital means beds, staff and ambulances — bought, and staffed, forever.
    label ccu_all "Every hospital: beds, staff" below #b5482e attach_to q_all

And every one of those beds comes from somewhere — from care we already know works, for patients nobody will ever name.
    highlight q_all effect glow

Cochrane actually named three tests — effectiveness, efficiency, and equality — and that third word came back in the 1990s, when Britain discovered it was rationing by postcode.
    label hta_label "HTA starts here" right attach_to q_all
    mark mark_hta target hta_label kind box

So the founding idea is this: proving a treatment works settles only half the decision — the other half is whether a health service can buy it for everyone who could benefit.
    delivery grave

## Where next
